# This scenario SIGKILLs a worker on purpose, so the master legitimately logs
#   [alert] <master>#0: worker process <pid> exited on signal 9
# which prober_scrape_log would otherwise treat as a server-side failure and red
# the whole scenario. Exempt exactly that line -- and ONLY that line.
#
# The regex is deliberately pinned to "exited on signal 9": a FAULT signal
# (SIGSEGV=11, SIGABRT=6, SIGBUS=7) carries an [alert] this pattern does NOT
# match, so it stays un-exempted and reds the log scrape. That is the backstop
# to the driver's oracle 3 (no OTHER signal death): a cascade fault reds here
# even if the driver's grep logic were ever wrong. Blanket-allowing all alerts
# would throw that backstop away.
#
# A SECOND signal-9 exit is NOT caught by this pattern -- "[0-9]+" matches every
# worker pid, so an extra "exited on signal 9" line is exempted here too. The
# protection against an unexpected additional SIGKILL is the driver's oracle 3
# count check (N_SIG9 > 1 reds), NOT this log exemption.
PROBER_ALLOW_LOG='worker process [0-9]+ exited on signal 9'
